arm: create 64-bit-shift scratch pseudos in a deterministic order

The ashldi3/ashrdi3/lshrdi3 expanders pass two gen_reg_rtx (SImode)
calls as arguments to arm_emit_coreregs_64bit_shift.  C++ argument
evaluation order is unspecified, so which scratch receives the lower
pseudo number depends on the compiler that built cc1: observed
right-to-left when cc1 is built on an x86_64 host and left-to-right on
an aarch64 host.  The swapped pseudo numbering survives into register
allocation, where it flips equal-cost tie-breaks: the practical symptom
is 12 differing bytes (pairs of swapped stack spill slots) in
std::from_chars in the target libstdc++.so, breaking byte-for-byte
reproducibility of ARM cross-builds across build-host architectures.

Hoist the two gen_reg_rtx calls into sequenced statements so pseudo
numbering -- and therefore generated code -- is host-independent.

Diagnosed on gcc 13.3.0 (buildroot internal toolchain, target
arm-buildroot-linux-gnueabihf): with this patch, x86_64- and
aarch64-hosted builds produce byte-identical libstdc++.so.6.0.32.
Same code is still present in gcc master as of 2026-07.

Kept in the unversioned patches/gcc/ dir deliberately: if a future gcc
bump includes an upstream fix, this patch will fail to apply and the
build will error loudly (instead of silently regressing cross-host
reproducibility, which no build error would ever catch).

--- a/gcc/config/arm/arm.md
+++ b/gcc/config/arm/arm.md
@@ -4600,9 +4600,17 @@
 	}
     }
 
+  /* Create the two scratch pseudos in a fixed order.  As arguments of
+     the call below, the two gen_reg_rtx calls' evaluation order would be
+     unspecified, making the pseudo register numbering depend on the
+     argument evaluation order of the compiler that built cc1 -- observed
+     to differ between x86_64 and aarch64 build hosts, breaking
+     cross-host-reproducible builds (12 differing bytes in the target's
+     libstdc++ std::from_chars, from swapped spill slots downstream).  */
+  rtx scratch1 = gen_reg_rtx (SImode);
+  rtx scratch2 = gen_reg_rtx (SImode);
   arm_emit_coreregs_64bit_shift (ASHIFT, operands[0], operands[1],
-				 operands[2], gen_reg_rtx (SImode),
-				 gen_reg_rtx (SImode));
+				 operands[2], scratch1, scratch2);
   DONE;
 ")
 
